  "content": {
    "id": "p54-q1-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "1",
    "prompt": "An arrow was shot into the air. The height, $h$ metres, of the arrow above the ground at any time, $t$ seconds, is given by the formula $h=-(t-6)^2+42$. The included sketch may be useful.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [
      {
        "id": "p54-q1-graph",
        "role": "question",
        "format": "tikz",
        "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"graph\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"source page 54 p54-q1-graph\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.38cm,y=0.055cm]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\draw[<->,line width=0.5pt] (-1.8,0)--(15,0) node[right] {$t$};\n\\draw[<->,line width=0.5pt] (0,-12)--(0,54) node[above] {$h$};\n\\draw[line width=0.8pt,domain=0:12.4807407,samples=100] plot (\\x,{-(\\x-6)^2+42});\n\\node[above] at (11,51) {$h=-(t-6)^2+42$};\n\\node[rotate=90,anchor=south] at (-1.8,25) {Height in metres};\n\\node[below] at (7.5,-12) {Time in seconds};\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
        "src": null,
        "widthMm": 70,
        "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
        "overlayOf": null,
        "transparent": false,
        "axes": null,
        "spec": {
          "sourcePage": 54,
          "sourceRegion": "Reference sketch beneath question 1 stem",
          "description": "Equation-derived downward-opening trajectory from the vertical axis to the positive ground intercept. Both axes have arrowheads at both ends; no ticks, numerical scale or shading. Source blue axes and yellow curve become ordinary black marks under house style.",
          "mathematics": "h=-(t-6)^2+42, displayed for 0≤t≤6+√42; vertex (6,42) and initial height 6 are represented by the curve but not numerically labelled.",
          "answerVisibility": "Permanent supplied reference graph visible in the student question."
        },
        "derived": false,
        "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
      }
    ],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p54-q1-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Using the vertex of this parabola, what was the maximum height reached by the arrow and at what time was this?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "42 m at 6 s.",
          "worked": "The vertex is $(6,42)$. The parabola opens downwards, so the maximum height is 42 m, reached at $t=6$ s.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p54-q1-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "From what height was the arrow initially fired? (Hint: ‘Initially’ means ‘at the start’, so $t=0$.)",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "6 m.",
          "worked": "Let $t=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}h&=-(0-6)^2+42\\\\h&=-36+42\\\\h&=6\\end{align*}$$\nThe initial height was 6 m.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p54-q1-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "How high above the ground was the arrow after 2 seconds?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "26 m.",
          "worked": "Let $t=2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}h&=-(2-6)^2+42\\\\h&=-16+42\\\\h&=26\\end{align*}$$\nThe arrow was 26 m above the ground.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p54-q1-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "How high above the ground was the arrow after 10 seconds?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "26 m.",
          "worked": "Let $t=10$.\n$$\\begin{align*}h&=-(10-6)^2+42\\\\h&=-16+42\\\\h&=26\\end{align*}$$\nThe arrow was 26 m above the ground.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p54-q1-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "How do the answers to (c) and (d) relate to the answers for (a)?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "6 s is the midpoint of 2 s and 10 s. Both heights are equal by symmetry about $t=6$.",
          "worked": "The maximum occurs at $t=6$ s, the midpoint of 2 s and 10 s. Both times are 4 seconds from the axis of symmetry $t=6$, so the arrow is at the same height, 26 m, at both times.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p54-q1-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "How long will it take the arrow to hit the ground? (Hint: what height is the ground?) Give your answer to 2 decimal places.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "12.48 s.",
          "worked": "At ground level, $h=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}0&=-(t-6)^2+42\\\\(t-6)^2&=42\\\\t-6&=\\pm\\sqrt{42}\\\\t&=6\\pm\\sqrt{42}\\\\t&\\approx-0.48\\text{ or }12.48\\end{align*}$$\nThe negative time is before the arrow was fired. It hits the ground after 12.48 s.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p54-q1-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "Another arrow’s height can be determined using the formula $h=-a(t-n)^2+p$, where $a$, $n$ and $p$ are unknown numbers.\n\nFind the equation of its path if the arrow was initially fired from a height of 1 metre and it reached a maximum height of 28 m after 9 seconds.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$h=-\\frac13(t-9)^2+28$.",
          "worked": "The vertex is $(9,28)$, so $n=9$ and $p=28$.\n$$h=-a(t-9)^2+28$$\nInitially, $t=0$ and $h=1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}1&=-a(0-9)^2+28\\\\1&=-81a+28\\\\81a&=27\\\\a&=\\frac13\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore,\n$$h=-\\frac13(t-9)^2+28.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 54
      }
    ]
  },
